From Richard.Jarrett at csiro.au  Sun Jun  2 22:00:56 2002
From: Richard.Jarrett at csiro.au
Date: Mon, 03 Jun 2002 08:00:56 +1000
Subject: Re: geometric mean
Message-ID: <CCB2683E47559142B2DFE0768181A5BB77C2CC@campaspe-cq.vic.cmis.CSIRO.AU>

> exp(Ybar-1.96 SE) to exp(Ybar+1.96 SE) is a 95% CI for exp(mu).
> But if X is log-normal, the mean of X, is not exp(mu).
> It is exp(mu + sigma-squared/2).
>If you are happy with the log-normal assumption, multiply the exponentiated
>CI endpoints by exp(s^2/2), where s^2 is the sample variance

Well, its approximate, but wrong because the errors due to the variance of
s^2 will typically be bigger than the errors due to the variance of  Ybar,
unless sigma^2 is quite small (<<1).

Consider Var(Ybar)=sigma^2/n, Var(s^2/2)=sigma^4/((2(n-1)), and these are
independent.
Hence the 95% ci for exp(mu + sigma^2/2) is
                exp(Ybar+s^2/2 +- 1.96SE) 
where           SE = sqrt(s^2/n+s^4/((2(n-1))).
Further complications:
(i) for small n, s^2 will be quite skew, so the symmetric conf interval on
the log scale will not be all that accurate, and
(ii) you would be best to use t(n-1,alpha) rather than 1.96.

Richard Jarrett
