Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.31.0206060805210.11195-100000@gannet.stats>

On Thu, 6 Jun 2002, Eric Elguero wrote:

[Quoting me out of context and without attribution.  That's a breach of
my copyright.]

> > S-PLUS does not have a missing value for character strings.  "NA" is not
> > missing, but e.g. a little-known continent.
>
> however, some functions recognize "NA" as missing, as example below
> shows (S+ 3.3 -  W95)
>
> >coxph(Surv(duree,typsortie==1)~SEX*(month<7),data=nes.7882.2002)
> Error in .C("coxfit2",: subroutine coxfit2: 2 missing value(s) in argument 6
> Dumped

SEX here is not a character vector, it is a factor, and factors do have
missing values.  (Character vectors in formulae make no sense.)  See
?factor for the conditions under which "NA" in a character string is
coerced to the missing value in a factor.

> > sum(is.na(nes.7802.2002$SEX))
> [1] 0
>
> > unique(nes.7802.2002$SEX)
> [1] "M"  "F"  "NA"
> >
>
> by the way, is there a canonical way to code missing values
> in character vectors? I was in trouble when I tried to read
> a variable describing people's activity where some values
> were missing (blank) and other were "NA" (no activity).

Use factor() explicitly.  As I said, character vectors DO NOT have missing
