> Is it possible to create data form a Gaussian distn where skewness and
> kurtosis will be different from 0 and 3 respectively ?
> (using S-plus of course).
> 
> Thomas.
        [WNV]  This is literally impossible with any software.  If the
skewness differs from 0 or the kurtosis (according to one definition) from 3
then the distribution is not Gaussian.  So you meant something else, I
presume.  There is not much point in saying too much before finding out
precisely what you do mean, but I offer two possible leads:

        1. mixtures of normal distributions are often useful in creating a
variety of near-normal distributions with given skewnesses and kurtoses and
they are dead simple to generate very fast.

        2. the Cornish-Fisher 'direct' (as opposed to 'inverse') expansions
can be used to generate a distribution with given cumulants (approximately,
but usually close enough) as a polynomial function of a normal variate, and
hence very simply and very fast.  (This is rather like using the Edgeworth
expansion to define the distribution.  It's fascinating old stuff but now
largely neglected, sadly.)

        Bill Venables

        [OK, so what do I mean by the '(according to one definition)'
enigmatic little aside above?  I prefer the definitions based on cumulants
rather than on moments, and for these the normal has skewness and kurtosis
BOTH zero.  Of course this is part of Fisher's angry revolt against the
Pearsonian tradition, but he had a point: cumulants are much simper to use
for this kind of thing than moments.]

