As others have pointed out, this is impossible as stated, by definition.  

But it got me thinking.  

Skewness is fairly intuitive, and it's usually easy to tell by eye whether a 
curve is left skew or right skew.  Kurtosis is much less intuitive.  

I've been playing around a little with mixture models, looking at how adding 
different "other" data changes the moments (mean, variance, skew, kurtosis) of 
normal curves.  But I've been doing it in a totally nonsystematic way.

Is there a "science" to this?  I would imagine quite a bit has been done, but I 
don't know the literature at all.....

Thanks

