Peter Flom asks:

>  -----Original Message-----
> From:         Peter Flom [mailto:flom at ndri.org] 
> Sent: Sunday, June 09, 2002 7:59 AM
> To:   s-news at lists.biostat.wustl.edu
> Subject:      Re: [S] a gaussian with skewness and kurtosis?
> 
> As others have pointed out, this is impossible as stated, by definition.  
> 
> But it got me thinking.  
> 
> Skewness is fairly intuitive, and it's usually easy to tell by eye whether
> a curve is left skew or right skew.  Kurtosis is much less intuitive.  
> 
> I've been playing around a little with mixture models, looking at how
> adding different "other" data changes the moments (mean, variance, skew,
> kurtosis) of normal curves.  But I've been doing it in a totally
> nonsystematic way.
> 
> Is there a "science" to this?  I would imagine quite a bit has been done,
> but I don't know the literature at all.....
        [WNV]  Hmm.  Sort of.  For mixtures are the moments about the origin
are simple to calculate.  In general (using a pretty obvious notation):

                mu_{mix}[j] = p1 mu1[j] + p2 mu2[j] + ...

        That is, the moments of the mixture about the origin are just linear
combinations of the moments of the individual components about the origin,
with coefficients the mixing proportions.  OK now suppose you stick to just
two gaussian components.  This gives you 5 parameters to play with (two
means, two variances and a mixing probability).  In principle you should be
able to get a distribution with the first 5 moments about the origin
specified.  Of course there are problems in trying to get the lot since the
solution of the equations you have to solve may give, e.g. negative
variances or mixing proportion outside (0,1), but you can still get a useful
set of distributions that do correspond to valid mixtures.  You do have to
solve a set of non-linear equations, too, but you only need do this once.
Simulation is then very fast and efficient, even using S code, (but it must
use ever vectorization feature you can lay your hands upon, of course.)  You
might also need to bone up a bit on going from moments about the origin to
moments about the mean, too.

        Not exactly a science but a pretty simple sort of strategy, at
least.

        Bill Venables.

