From davidov at stat.haifa.ac.il  Sun Jun  9 14:02:19 2002
From: davidov at stat.haifa.ac.il (Ori Davidov)
Date: Sun, 09 Jun 2002 16:02:19 +0200
Subject: Help with GEE - summary
Message-ID: <011b01c20fbe$45ac0720$c34b4a84@uri-d.hevra.haifa.ac.il>

Hi,

 

I would like to thank Brian Ripley, Frank 
Harrell, Renaud Lancelot and Jagadish Rangrej for their help. My query was: 

 

>

>I am interested in analyzing correlated binary 
data, where each cluster is of size two. The data has the following structure

 

>Y       cID     X1,....,XN

>1        1

>0        1

>0        2

>0        2

 

>where Y is the binary outcome and cID is the 
cluster number and X1 through XN are explanatory covariates. Observations within 
each >cluster are assumed to be dependent.

 

>I have looked at the help menu for 
quasi-likelihood methods without much success. Thanks for your 
help,

 

There are basically tewo alternatives: 

 

(1)

 

quasi-likelihood is not the same as gee. For gee models, see gee and yags 
library, written by Vince Carey and available on the StatLib or
directly on 
his web page:

http://biosun1.harvard.edu/~carey/index.ssoft.html

quasi 
models are handled with glm and family quasi. See help for glm and 
family.

For a gee implementation of your problem, you could do something 
like:

library(gee)
attach(mydata) ### your data.frame
fm <- 
gee(Y ~ X1 + X2 + XN, id = cID, family = binomial(logit), corstr 
=
"exchangeable")
detach(mydata)
summary(gee)

or

 

(2)

 

A GEE-type approach with coefficients estimated from a working independence 
model can work quite well for your situation.  One such approach is to use 
the Design library's lrm function, then pass the fit object to the robcov or 
bootcov function which both implement intra-cluster correlation adjustments for 
the variance-covariance matrix.  I think that the cluster bootstrap works a 
little better than the cluster sandwich estimator (robcov).  See the help 
file in robcov or bootcov for an example like yours.  -Frank 
Harrell

