From BobBaskin at westat.com  Thu Jun 13 17:57:27 2002
From: BobBaskin at westat.com (Bob Baskin)
Date: Thu, 13 Jun 2002 13:57:27 -0400
Subject: Re: is it wishart
In-Reply-To: <CKBKHMMGKLIIPAAA@mailcity.com>
Message-ID: <15104802790BD411A2C100D0B73EA33C02DED8D4@remailnt3-re01.westat.com>

i assume your a1...an are known constants.  if the a_i are all equal then
you have a distribution proportional to a chi-squared distribution.

i think technically you can hammer your setup into a singular wishart
distribution with 1 df, but i don't know what good it would do you.  if Zi
are iid n(0,1) then your X = sum(a_i*Zi**2) = trace(W) where W is the
wishart with 1 df (here W(i,j)=sqrt(a_i)Zi*sqrt(a_j)Zj).

you can also think of your X as a weighted sum of squares and possibly force
them into a weighted least squares setup where the covariance matrix is
diagonal.  i think there is something like this in seber's book on linear
regression (i don't have a copy handy) and probably a hundred other books on
regression.

and finally, you may be able to do enough yoga to shape your X into a form
like a Wald statistic, but offhand i don't know how you would proceed from
there.

just wild guesses
bob

