Stanley Rubenstein asks:

>  -----Original Message-----
> From:         Stanley Ribinstein [mailto:srubins at lycos.com] 
> Sent: Friday, June 14, 2002 2:05 AM
> To:   S-PLUS
> Subject:      [S] is it wishart
> 
> Dear S-PLUS statistical gurus:
> I  have a kind of simple question, but failed to find a direct answer in
> the textbooks.
> Suppose I have a sequence of N normal independent iid's with all means 0
> and all variances 1. 
> 
> I need to know the distribution of X=a1*x1^2+...+aN*xn^2. Coefficients
> a1...an
> are positive and in general all diferent.  Is it a known distribution? Can
> it be expressed through  
> wishart distribution? 
        [WNV]  Not unless the coefficients are all 1 when it has a
chi-squared distribution, a kind of degenerate special case of Wishart. 

        In general this is just a quadratic form in normal variables.  There
is an enormous amount of work done on distributional problems for these, but
the results are not simple.  It is one of those situations where you can get
the characteristic function of the distribution, and hence the moments,
relatively easily, but getting a hold of the density itself in manageable
form is not easy.  

        One good reference on this, curiously, is the now quite old book on
Regression by R. L. Plackett, but best of luck finding it in any modern
library!  There is also an excellent old paper by Robbins and Pitman on
mixture representations of the distributions in the Annals of Mathematical
Statistics about 1947 or 8, that I used to use quite a bit when I needed
this kind of result.

