From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Fri Jun 14 14:45:12 2002
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk
Date: Fri, 14 Jun 2002 15:45:12 +0100
Subject: Re: Mallows Cp and AIC commands for whole model
In-Reply-To: <3D09FE0B.F735DF62@ucalgary.ca>
References: <3D09FE0B.F735DF62@ucalgary.ca>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.31.0206141537450.7560-100000@gannet.stats>

On Fri, 14 Jun 2002, Julia Linke wrote:

> I have been working with generalized linear models in explaining
> movement rates with a set of environmental variables.  I have computed
> CP values (thinking that they more or less equate AIC values) for each

They don't come close to equating to AIC values.  That's a serious
problem with the use of step, add1 and drop1 on glm objects.

The functions in the MASS library, addterm, dropterm and stepAIC, do this
correctly.

> covariate/predictor of a model to find out which predictors are most
> important, using the following two commands loading the mass library
> first (Splus 2000 Professional Release 2):
> 1) movement.null<-glm(AVE.MOVE~1,family=poisson,data=movement)
> 2) add1(movement.null,~.+areamn.stand + shapemn.stand + enncv.stand +
> siei.stand + pland123.stand + pland45.stand + pland7.stand +
> pland89.stand + pland11.stand + pland13.stand + pland14.stand +
> pland15.stand + pland16.stand + river.stand)
>
> My question:
> 1) How different is Mallows Cp from AIC values as Burnham and Anderson
> (1998) indicate?

Let's take Akaike as the authority, not B&A!  The difference is explained
in Venables & Ripley Chapter 6 for linear models, and is usually not
great.  But for glm's, add1 is using Cp on the linear approximation, and
that can be way off.

(Beware: parts of S-PLUS use an AIC due to Hastie, not the original due to
Akaike.  I don't know if B&A use the original definitions or not.)

> 2) Would someone have a command to compute CPs and/or AIC values for the
> whole model instead of each variable (such as for example in Chapter
> 3.2.  in Burnham and Anderson 1998).  I would really appreciate it if
> someone could help me.

It's all in Venables & Ripley, with worked examples.

