Dear François-Léonce,

I am not sure whether this is the answer to your problems, but the same
issue occurred in my work some months ago. There are a couple of ways to
export graphs to files and keep written track of what you have done to come
that far. see for instance ?export.graph() and ?postscript() functions,
especially export.graph has options for various file types of graphs. For my
type of work, I find postscript very helpful, they are easy to
handle,exchange and copy-paste.

this the simpliest form of my solution:

## switches postscript on to place your plot in c:temp, horizontal controls
portrait(F) or landscape(T)
postscript("C:\\Temp\\yourpicturename.ps", horizontal=F)  
plot(x,y)    ## here you can plot as many things as you wish and/or write
text
dev.off()  ## this switches off the postscript, After this, graphs go to the
GSDx again

# To get the date and time of creation in your report, use for instance:

paste("this S-plus graph was created on",date())

# maybe you want to get colors in the postscript graph as well: let the
postscript command be preceded by:

ps.options(paper="A4",colors=ps.colors.rgb[c("black","red",
"blue","green"),]) 

# paper sets printer output right: Europe A4, US sometimes Letter
# the order of colors in the vector above correspond to color numbers
1,2,3,4,....etc.. used in plot commands)

If you insert a mtext(date(), <<proper location>>) statement between
postscript and dev.off(), then the date is plotted on your graph as well,
which makes it perfect to check that the creation date denoted in your
report matches the one on your graph. After you have run the program, you
can save your output window as report text file. The bad news of it all is,
that you can easily change and write in the reports, in contrast with SAS
logs that can be made read-only. Therefore, one can never be entirely sure
that reports report the truth. Perhaps somebody else can comment on that.

Hope this helps
Klaas

